A||||16||batboy||Jack for lowered Fieros?||||1||
Z||000000||batboy||06-07-2000||06:10 PM||||Now that I've lowered the Batmobile, I'm having trouble finding a compact jack that I can use on the front end. Once I install my front air dam it'll be dang near impossible. Right now I'm using a couple 2" X 6" blocks of wood to drive the front tires up onto, then using my regular hydraulic floor jack. Any suggestions? Even scizzor jacks I've looked at will only go down to about 5" which won't fit. Ideas?||||reg||1
Z||000001||lawrence||06-07-2000||07:03 PM||||Since I installed my air dam, I have the same problem -- and the same solution.  I have four 2X12 by 2' that I drive on before I roll the floor jack under it.  I'm trying to think of a good way to put some hydraulic pistons in the car ala the Indy cars.  Too bad we don't have a power steering pump to use for pressure.<P>-L <A HREF="http://members.nccw.net/webe" TARGET=_blank>http://members.nccw.net/webe</A>  - WebElectric Magazine||||reg||1
Z||000002||N_Man||06-07-2000||08:52 PM||||Might be more trouble than its worth but if you jack up one side of the rear it lifts the front enough to squeeze most jacks under the car.||||reg||1
Z||000003||terryk||06-07-2000||08:56 PM||||A lower jack....<P>I finally went to a wrecker and got a spare U-van jack and lift the one side of the car enough to get my short little jack under there.<P>Eibach are the reason......<P>I got a U-van jack because it uses a 19mm bolt to raise/lower it but has the same chassis contact as the Fiero. I can hit it with my air wrench just before I pop the wheels off.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||batboy||06-07-2000||09:51 PM||||Duh, what's a U-van? Regardless, I think Terry has the right idea. I need to find a jack that fits the same side jacking point, raise that enough to slide my floor jack in under the front. Still kind of sux having to use two jacks.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dan86gt3.8||06-07-2000||11:22 PM||||damn its hard enought to do it on a stock hieght fiero, much less a lowered one!||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fret Naught||06-08-2000||12:14 AM||||Nice to see that we are all on the same page, those two 2x6 blocks fit real nice in my trunk and take up very little room. I figure heck I got enough stuff to worry about breaking or buying, why sweat the small stuff||||reg||1
Z||000007||Oreif||06-08-2000||02:47 AM||||I use the block method also. It was kind of a shock when I first lowered my car after the suspension was completed and I couldn't get the floor jack out! If anyone finds a jack that works let me know.||||reg||1
Z||000008||DiggityDan||06-08-2000||02:54 AM||||When I have to work on the front side....I always jack the back up first, put it on stands and then I stick a jack from behind the back wheel onto the frame area there on the front. This usually lifts the front without interfering with the back if it hit it in the right place. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||fierodave||06-08-2000||05:31 AM||||get one of them inflatable  jacks I think JC whitney sells<BR>they have a built in pump and it runs off the cig lighter power  same concept fire fighters and emergincy people use to jack up cars off people in accidents.<BR>I have seen them lift more than a corner of a fiero<BR>FD||||reg||1
Z||000010||lennyb4||06-08-2000||08:05 AM||||Hey guys.. Check out the jacks that came with cars like the 88-91 beretta. They lower down to a point of around 2 1/2 to 3". I have one in my garage and ive seen them alot in the bone yards.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000011||Spyder||06-08-2000||10:14 AM||||To lift my front using a hydraulic floor jack, which is the narrow type, I turn the steering wheel to open up the wheel well and then lift on the a-arm. Put in a jack stand then lift on the center cross member.||||reg||1
Z||000012||batboy||06-08-2000||03:47 PM||||Spyder, I tried that too. You must have a lower floor jack than me. I have 88 Grand Am/Beretta brakes, maybe I'll look into a jack from that car too. I'm assuming the Grand Am would be the same. Otherwise, maybe the wood blocks are a good idea after all. I thought it sounded very "hickish" and low tech, but if it works, what the hey? I fear having a flat on the road and not being able to change it. Didn't think about taking the blocks with me, duh!||||reg||1
Z||000013||lowCG||06-09-2000||09:29 PM||||Sounds like the Baretta jack would work for emergencies,I used to have an old Fiat jack that worked great until one day...<BR>I also have an old JCPenney floor jack that will fit under the car when I took the cup off,and put a 5/16 bolt through the hole for a register.<BR>The el-cheapo hydraulic jacks will fit too,if you're not on the bumpstops,which I hope your not.||||reg||1
Z||000014||batboy||06-09-2000||11:22 PM||||No, I'm not on the bump stops, but I still can't find a hydraulic jack that will slide in under the front. The rear end is no problem. The Fiero jack goes in ok at the normal side jacking point right now, but it might not if I have a completely flat tire. Guess I'd better carry those wood blocks with me.||||reg||1
Z||000015||terryk||06-09-2000||11:28 PM||||U body van is Chevy Lumina APV, Pontiac Trans Sport and Olds Silhouette Van.<P>The jacks are the same as the Fiero, but with a 19mm nut rather than a handle.||||reg||1
Z||000016||lowCG||06-10-2000||01:40 PM||||I forgot to mention that I get under the front by turning the wheels all the way towards that side,then go in front of the turned tire/wheel with the jack and catch the crossmember.||||reg||1
